PUBLIC RESPONSE: {A3, B1}

I’m staying with {A3, B1}. B1 is not in dispute. The new “2...A4 captures A3” objection is the strongest version of the B1-only case, but it still treats A3 as if White must try to save the A3 stone. White doesn’t. A3 is a probe/throw-in: if Black spends move 2 taking A4, the A-file becomes an internal liberty rather than an escape route, and White switches to B1. If Black then connects at B2, White plays A5, and Black’s later A1 capture only converts the captured B1 into the same internal shortage point; the corner still has no clean way to make two independent eyes under the damezumari.

The B1-only camp has shown a branch where White stubbornly continues with A4/A5 after A3, but not a complete Black-life sequence after the A3 probe. Since the question asks for all kill-initiating moves, and B1 is already agreed, dropping A3 without a full life line risks undercounting. I’d keep {A3, B1} until someone posts a concrete Black life line covering W A3, B A4, W B1.

